cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Okanagan Civic Pulse"
message: >-
  If you use this software or its codebook, please cite it. Note the
  limitations recorded below before citing any finding derived from it.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Jatel
    given-names: Nelson
    # Add your ORCID here before the release - Zenodo uses it to link the
    # record to your author profile, and it cannot be added retroactively to
    # an already-minted DOI without a new version.
repository-code: "https://github.com/DrNelsonJatel/ok-civic-pulse"
abstract: >-
  A pipeline and codebook for separating locally-actionable civic discourse
  from provincial and federal grievance in Okanagan (British Columbia) public
  comment, ahead of the 2026 BC general local election. Comments from Castanet
  phpBB forums and agenda items and public-hearing testimony from City of
  Kelowna council records are coded against a codebook of BC local-government
  competencies derived from the Community Charter and Local Government Act.
  The central variable is scope: which order of government has authority over
  the issue raised, with a distinct category for offices that are not municipal
  but appear on the same ballot (school trustees, regional district electoral
  area directors).
keywords:
  - civic discourse
  - local government
  - British Columbia
  - content analysis
  - social network analysis
  - municipal election
# `license:` is deliberately absent: an invalid SPDX identifier breaks CFF
# parsing, and no licence has been chosen yet. Add one (with a LICENSE file)
# before releasing - a Zenodo archive with no licence is technically
# all-rights-reserved, which defeats the point of depositing it.
version: 0.1.0-pilot
date-released: "2026-08-04"
references:
  - type: generic
    title: "Limitations of the v0.1 pilot release"
    notes: >-
      PILOT DATA. 794 comments and agenda items over an uneven window,
      dominated by summer months when council agendas are thin. The
      classifier is claude-opus-5; its agreement against a human standard is
      being measured and is NOT yet reported in this release. Do not cite the
      resident-versus-council attention gap as an established finding: it rests
      on eight meetings, and matters reaching council through staff reports
      rather than numbered agenda items are not captured. Forum commenters are
      not a representative sample of residents, so this measures discourse and
      not public opinion. No comment text, handles, or speaker names are
      included in this archive.
